Subject: [PATCH 0037] inter: only clear xd->mv_refined when optical flow can read it

av2_build_inter_predictors() opens with

    if (plane == AVM_PLANE_Y)
      memset(xd->mv_refined, 0, 2 * N_OF_OFFSETS * sizeof(int_mv));

xd->mv_refined is 2 * N_OF_OFFSETS int_mv = 8192 bytes, sized for the
optical-flow subblock grid of a whole 256x256 superblock. It is cleared on
every luma call, so an 8x8 inter block pays all 8192 bytes.

This is the single largest memset in the encoder. From callgrind on the
current tip (320x192, 3 frames, 1 thread, qp=160):

    cpu-used=5   4.28% of retired instructions
    cpu-used=2   4.65% of retired instructions   (2nd largest line overall)

WHAT READS IT

Inside this function's call tree, every reader of xd->mv_refined sits under
use_optflow_refinement, computed in
build_inter_predictors_8x8_and_bigger_facade() as

    is_optflow_refinement_enabled(cm, xd, mi, plane, tip_ref_frame)

together with the TIP writes at plane 0. The other branch of this function,
build_inter_predictors_sub8x8(), does not reference mv_refined at all.

So the guard is that predicate, hoisted to the entry point:

  * Non-TIP: is_optflow_refinement_enabled() reduces to
    opfl_allowed_cur_pred_mode(cm, xd, mi), which does NOT depend on plane.
    Its value at AVM_PLANE_Y is therefore the value the chroma passes of the
    same mi will compute, so clearing at plane 0 under the plane-0 value of
    the predicate covers every reader of that block.

  * TIP: the predicate does depend on plane (it returns ... && plane == 0),
    and av2/common/tip.c additionally carries xd->mv_refined[0..1] across
    call boundaries of its own. Rather than reason about that, the guard
    forces the memset on whenever is_tip_ref_frame(mi->ref_frame[0]), which
    is conservative and leaves the TIP path exactly as it is today.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM

This is a "written iff read" argument, and it is weaker than patch 0036's.
0036 guards function-local stack buffers, so nothing can observe the skipped
writes. xd->mv_refined is MACROBLOCKD state that persists across blocks, so
the argument depends on the claim that no reader runs with the predicate
false. That claim is made from reading the call tree, and the case that
would break it is a block whose chroma pass evaluates the predicate true
while its luma pass evaluated it false -- which the non-TIP plane
independence and the TIP force-on are intended to exclude.

Note that today's memset does not protect against cross-block staleness
either: a chroma call whose mi had no plane-0 call already sees whatever the
previous block left behind, since the memset is itself gated on
plane == AVM_PLANE_Y.

Because the argument is a reading of the code rather than a locality
property, treat the empirical bit-exactness sweep as the evidence, not as a
formality, and widen it before landing. In particular a configuration
mixing SDP (semi-decoupled partitioning) with OPFL is the one to add.

VERIFICATION

Bit-exactness was checked by .obu md5 against the unpatched build over
clip x preset x qindex, with 6-10 frame runs rather than the usual 2-4 so
that inter prediction, OPFL and TIP actually fire. Results are recorded in
experiments/registry.csv.

MEASURING THE SPEEDUP

Retired-instruction counts under callgrind, not wall clock -- the local
noise floor is several percent. Instruction counts understate this one,
because the work removed is memory traffic.

